    "lawCode": "Amd §§2 & 14, Emerg Ten Prot Act of 1974",
    "lawSection": "Emergency Tenant Protection Act of 1974",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5040\n\nSPONSOR: BRESLIN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to establish the \"statewide tenant protection\nact of 2019\"; and to amend the emergency tenant protection act of nine-\nteen seventy-four, in relation to expanding rent and eviction\nprotections statewide\n\n \nPURPOSE OF GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo remove arbitrary geographic restrictions on applicability of the\nstate's rent and eviction regulation system, thereby allowing munici-\npalities in all parts of the state to opt into the system where the\nmunicipality has declared a housing emergency based on a vacancy rate of\nfive percent or less.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 14 of section 4 of chapter 576 of the laws of 1974, constituting\nthe emergency tenant protection act of nineteen seventy-four, is\namended.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis legislation corrects an injustice. When the Emergency Tenant\nProtection Act of 1974 was enacted, its applicability was restricted to\nNew York City and municipalities in Nassau, Rockland and Westchester\nCounties that are eligible to opt into the state's system of regulating\nrents and evictions in buildings with six or more units constructed\nprior to 1974, after conducting a survey to determine if the vacancy\nrate is five percent or less.\n\nIn the intervening 45 years the need for rent and eviction protections\nacross the state has greatly increased. Removing the arbitrary geograph-\nic restrictions gives towns, villages and cities in all parts of the\nstate the right to opt into the system if the vacancy rate is low. This\nbill does not impose rent and eviction regulation on any locality; it is\nlocal option. Tenants need and deserve protection regardless of where\nthey live, and municipalities deserve the right to opt into the ETPA if\nthey choose to do so.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nThe NYS Division of Housing and Community Renewal might need to open\nsome additional district rent offices in parts of the state that are not\ncurrently covered by the ETPA. Some of this cost would be supported by\nthe $10 per unit annual fee that municipalities must pay to the state,\nand which municipalities can recover from the owners of apartments\nsubject to the ETPA.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
